[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What marks a task as one you should be reminded about? → Has a due date (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m I have enough to design. I'll present it in sections and check with you after each rather than dropping the whole
  thing at once.

  [1mSection 1 — Scope and sequencing[0m

  [1mWhat we're building:[0m a single-user, browser-only task app whose tasks carry due dates, with reminders delivered live
  while open and caught up on next open. No server, no build step, no accounts.

  The notifications system you asked for is the third of three layers, and the first two don't exist. So the work
  sequences as:

  - [1mPhase 1 — Tasks core.[0m Add a task, list tasks, mark complete, delete. Persist to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m. Renders into the
    [38;5;153m<main>[39m that's currently empty. This is the foundation the rest is meaningless without.
  - [1mPhase 2 — Due dates.[0m An optional due date per task, displayed in the list, with overdue visually distinct. Small,
    but it's what makes "time passing" a real signal.
  - [1mPhase 3 — Reminders.[0m The actual notifications system: the due-check ticker, live delivery, catch-up on open,
    permission handling, and the in-page fallback.

  This is small enough to be [1mone spec with three phases[0m, not three separate projects. I'd keep them as distinct phases
  in the implementation plan so phase 1 is working and verifiable before anything notification-shaped gets layered on.

  [1mDeliberately out of scope[0m — flagging these so they're your call, not my silent omission: accounts, sync, or sharing;
  recurring tasks; snooze; priorities, tags, or projects; search and filtering beyond an overdue view; editing a task's
  title after creation (delete-and-re-add covers it); any framework, bundler, or build step. Each is cheap to add later
  against this model, and none of them is needed for reminders to work.

  Does that scope and sequencing look right — and is there anything in that out-of-scope list you actually want pulled
  in?

